the day begins in dark ooh with the  arrival of the morning school boat the  children live on outlying islands in the  rail river delta lessons will begin soon  a drumbeat signals the start of school  today's lesson plan includes civics  don't wait for others to do something  for you think instead about how you can  be useful to the community otherwise  you're a silliest breadfruit community  is important here in dark uu a tiny  village in the southeast of Fiji's main  island Viti Levu the 400 some villagers  have a common problem it has become  nearly impossible to grow anything here  as a result of climate change  the river rises on a regular basis and  floods half the village that's why our  houses are on stilts the brackish water  rises from below we can't plant  vegetables because everything dies right  away it's become more extreme over the  past eight years recently a dam was  built to protect the village it was paid  for with government and international  aid without here we might have been  forced to move away we want to save the  village for our children and  grandchildren but how can the village  sustain itself the women of dark who  have found an answer and simultaneously  sparked a revolution for generations  they've been weaving fans out of palm  fronds their technique is a well-kept  secret  it's something sacred  a village that was granted to us by Viva  Luigi's first king we weren't ever  allowed to sell the fans but now with  climate change and the economic problems  our men requested permission from the  king and for the past few years we've  been allowed to sell them the fans are  sold for about three euros each and now  account for nearly 70% of dark whose  income financially the men of dark who  are now almost completely dependent on  the women it's a major change in their  lives until recently women and younger  villagers had little say the Fiji  Islands are still largely a  male-dominated society that's abundantly  clear at the several civil ceremony only  men get to drink the coveted kava root  grog it's bitter numbs the mouth and  it's considered a real delicacy the  drink is also said to be healthy the  woven fan initiative hasn't led to  greater social upheaval in the village  thanks to a gender equality training  program financed with German funding  villagers attended workshops where they  learned that they could solve their  economic problems better if the men and  women work together as equals the  women's representative tries to ensure  that the lesson isn't forgotten  but not everyone in the village is happy  when the bastions of male dominance are  threatened the school manager a  respected member of the community airs  his complaints for me gender has nothing  to do with equality the man should make  the decisions not the woman we know that  from the Bible the woman should obey and  serve the man that's the natural  god-given order and that shouldn't  change the men here have fished in the  rail River Delta for generations  these mangrove swamps were once swarming  with fish and shrimp but in many areas  deforestation has led to riverbank  erosion the water contains more sediment  the silt clothes the mangroves and  destroys the fish breeding places the  fishermen see the effects every time  they take their boats out the catch in  the fish traps is meager so the men now  regularly make trips to the fertile  island of khalili to collect palm leaves  for the fans everything that grows is  considered common property I bring  coconuts to eat back from the plantation  and branches that are used to make the  fans that's become our most important  source of income over the world it's a  well-organized supply system that gives  the men of dark ooh the opportunity to  contribute to the villagers success they  also help boil the fronds from the  coconut palms as husband of the women's  representative it's my job to support my  wife i seated the firewood in the water  and I'm very proud that she has this  important position glad you recognized  my word such examples of mutual respect  and not to be taken for granted in Fiji  meanwhile in dark ooh the villagers have  gathered for a celebration the highlight  is the Miki dance faces and bodies are  blackened with charcoal  here at least the young men of the  village can carry on male traditions  just like they did a hundred years ago
 

